    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "The pecking order theory of capital structure suggests firms prefer to finance new investments using:", "a": "New equity first, then debt, then retained earnings", "b": "Retained earnings first, then debt, then new equity as a last resort", "c": "Debt at all times to maximise tax shields", "d": "Equity to minimise financial distress risk", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Pecking order theory states that firms prefer internal funds, then external debt, and finally new equity (most costly due to information asymmetry).", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "Economic Value Added (EVA) measures:", "a": "The market value of the firm's equity", "b": "The profit earned above the cost of capital employed (NOPAT - WACC × Capital)", "c": "Total assets minus total liabilities", "d": "The annual cash dividend paid to shareholders", "answer": "b", "explanation": "EVA = NOPAT - (WACC × Invested Capital); positive EVA means the firm is creating value above its cost of capital.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "A company's WACC is 12%. It should accept a project if:", "a": "The project's payback period is less than 3 years", "b": "The project's IRR is greater than 12%", "c": "The project generates any positive cash flows", "d": "The project's return on assets exceeds 10%", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A project should be accepted if its IRR exceeds WACC, ensuring value creation for shareholders.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "The dividend irrelevance theory by Miller and Modigliani states:", "a": "Dividends always increase stock price", "b": "In perfect markets, dividend policy has no effect on firm value", "c": "Firms should never pay dividends", "d": "High dividend payout ratios attract more institutional investors", "answer": "b", "explanation": "MM dividend irrelevance holds that investors can replicate any dividend policy by selling or buying shares; value is unaffected.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "Capital rationing occurs when:", "a": "A company has unlimited funds for investment", "b": "A firm has more viable positive-NPV projects than available capital to fund them", "c": "The government restricts corporate borrowing", "d": "Interest rates are too high for project financing", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Capital rationing requires firms to rank and select projects when available capital is insufficient for all positive-NPV investments.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "The break-even point in capital budgeting analysis is the level of sales at which:", "a": "NPV equals the IRR", "b": "Total revenues equal total costs (both fixed and variable)", "c": "The firm generates its target return on equity", "d": "Cash flow from operations equals capital expenditure", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Break-even is where total revenue equals total costs; below it the firm makes a loss, above it makes a profit.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "Sensitivity analysis in capital budgeting examines:", "a": "How NPV changes when one variable is altered while holding others constant", "b": "The probability distribution of all possible outcomes simultaneously", "c": "Only the best-case and worst-case scenarios", "d": "The impact of tax rate changes on cash flows only", "answer": "a", "explanation": "Sensitivity analysis tests the impact of changing a single input variable (e.g., sales volume, price) on project NPV.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "The cost of debt used in WACC is typically calculated on an after-tax basis because:", "a": "Interest income is taxed in the hands of bondholders", "b": "Interest payments are tax-deductible, reducing the effective cost to the firm", "c": "Debt is always cheaper than equity before tax", "d": "Tax authorities refund half the interest paid", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Interest is tax-deductible, so the after-tax cost of debt = Pre-tax cost × (1 - Tax rate).", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "Real options in capital budgeting refer to:", "a": "Options to purchase real estate as part of a project", "b": "Managerial flexibility to adjust, expand, delay, or abandon a project based on future conditions", "c": "Currency options used to hedge project cash flows", "d": "The option to issue bonds rather than equity", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Real options recognise management's ability to adapt decisions in response to changing market conditions, adding value beyond static NPV.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "A company's degree of operating leverage (DOL) is high when:", "a": "It has high variable costs and low fixed costs", "b": "It has high fixed costs relative to variable costs, making profits highly sensitive to revenue changes", "c": "It uses a high proportion of debt financing", "d": "Its interest coverage ratio is above 5x", "answer": "b", "explanation": "High DOL means a large proportion of costs are fixed; small revenue changes cause large swings in operating profit.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "Working capital management focuses on:", "a": "Long-term investment decisions", "b": "Managing current assets and current liabilities to ensure operational liquidity", "c": "Setting the company's dividend policy", "d": "Determining the optimal capital structure", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Working capital management balances liquidity and profitability through effective management of receivables, payables, and inventory.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "Which of the following best represents free cash flow to equity (FCFE)?", "a": "Net income + Depreciation - Capital expenditure - Change in working capital", "b": "NOPAT - Capital expenditure - Change in working capital", "c": "Net income + Depreciation - Capital expenditure - Change in working capital + Net borrowing", "d": "Operating cash flow - Dividends paid", "answer": "c", "explanation": "FCFE = Net income + D&A - CapEx - ΔNWC + Net borrowing; it is the cash available to equity holders after debt obligations.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "Agency costs arise from conflicts between:", "a": "Regulators and companies", "b": "Shareholders (principals) and managers (agents) whose interests may diverge", "c": "Debt holders and equity holders", "d": "All of the above", "answer": "d", "explanation": "Agency costs arise in all principal-agent relationships including shareholders vs. managers, and debtholders vs. shareholders.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "The profitability index (PI) of a project is calculated as:", "a": "NPV / Initial investment", "b": "PV of future cash flows / Initial investment", "c": "IRR / WACC", "d": "Net income / Total assets", "answer": "b", "explanation": "PI = PV of future cash flows / Initial investment; PI > 1 means the project creates value (equivalent to NPV > 0).", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "In Ghana, a company's cost of equity is likely to be higher than in a developed market primarily because:", "a": "Ghanaian companies pay higher dividends", "b": "Higher country risk and inflation risk demand a higher equity risk premium", "c": "Ghana's stock exchange is more liquid", "d": "Corporate tax rates in Ghana are lower", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Emerging market equities carry higher risk premiums due to political, currency, and economic volatility, raising the cost of equity.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Corporate Finance", "chapter_num": 1, "question": "Scenario analysis in project evaluation differs from sensitivity analysis in that:", "a": "It changes one variable at a time", "b": "It simultaneously changes multiple variables to model best, base, and worst-case outcomes", "c": "It uses only historical data", "d": "It is only used for real estate projects", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Scenario analysis examines the impact of multiple simultaneous variable changes, unlike sensitivity analysis which isolates one variable.", "difficulty": "Medium"},

    # Chapter 2: Financial Statement Analysis
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "The DuPont analysis decomposes Return on Equity (ROE) into:", "a": "Profit margin, asset turnover, and financial leverage", "b": "Gross margin, operating margin, and net margin", "c": "Current ratio, quick ratio, and cash ratio", "d": "P/E ratio, P/B ratio, and EV/EBITDA", "answer": "a", "explanation": "DuPont ROE = Net profit margin × Asset turnover × Equity multiplier (financial leverage).", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Which of the following signals a potential earnings quality concern?", "a": "Net income closely tracking operating cash flow", "b": "Net income consistently and significantly exceeding operating cash flow", "c": "Stable revenue growth over multiple years", "d": "Conservative depreciation policies", "answer": "b", "explanation": "When net income persistently exceeds cash from operations, it may indicate aggressive revenue recognition or expense deferral.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Horizontal analysis of financial statements involves:", "a": "Expressing all line items as a percentage of total revenue or assets", "b": "Comparing financial data across multiple years to identify trends and changes", "c": "Comparing a company's ratios against industry peers", "d": "Analysing the balance sheet structure at a single point in time", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Horizontal (trend) analysis compares financial data across periods to identify growth patterns and anomalies.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Vertical (common-size) analysis expresses financial statement items as:", "a": "Year-on-year percentage changes", "b": "A percentage of a base figure (e.g., revenue for income statement, total assets for balance sheet)", "c": "Absolute monetary values adjusted for inflation", "d": "Multiples of industry average values", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Vertical analysis normalises financial statements by expressing items as percentages of a base, enabling cross-company comparison.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "A declining asset turnover ratio over time may indicate:", "a": "Improved operational efficiency", "b": "Overinvestment in assets or declining revenue relative to the asset base", "c": "Better working capital management", "d": "Higher profitability", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Falling asset turnover suggests assets are not being used as efficiently to generate revenue.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Operating leverage is reflected in a company's income statement by:", "a": "The proportion of debt in the capital structure", "b": "The proportion of fixed costs to total costs — high fixed costs amplify profit changes", "c": "The interest expense relative to EBIT", "d": "The tax rate applied to pre-tax income", "answer": "b", "explanation": "High operating leverage (high fixed costs) means revenue changes cause disproportionately large swings in operating profit.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "The cash conversion cycle (CCC) is calculated as:", "a": "Debtor days + Inventory days - Creditor days", "b": "Debtor days - Inventory days + Creditor days", "c": "Inventory days only", "d": "Creditor days - Debtor days", "answer": "a", "explanation": "CCC = Days Inventory Outstanding + Days Sales Outstanding - Days Payable Outstanding; measures liquidity efficiency.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Which ratio is most useful for comparing profitability between companies with different capital structures?", "a": "Return on equity (ROE)", "b": "Net profit margin", "c": "Return on invested capital (ROIC) or EBIT margin", "d": "Earnings per share (EPS)", "answer": "c", "explanation": "ROIC and EBIT margin are capital-structure-neutral metrics, allowing fairer comparison across firms with different leverage.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Goodwill impairment in financial statements indicates:", "a": "The company has grown through organic expansion", "b": "The carrying value of an acquired business exceeds its recoverable amount", "c": "The company's brand value has increased", "d": "A dividend has been paid from capital reserves", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Goodwill impairment occurs when the recoverable amount of an acquisition falls below the carrying value, indicating overpayment or deteriorating performance.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Off-balance-sheet financing is a concern for analysts because:", "a": "It overstates the company's profitability", "b": "It hides liabilities and leverage, making the balance sheet appear stronger than it is", "c": "It reduces reported revenues", "d": "It always involves illegal accounting practices", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Off-balance-sheet items (e.g., operating leases under old standards, special purpose vehicles) can conceal true leverage.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Which of the following would increase reported net income without a corresponding cash increase?", "a": "Early revenue recognition on a multi-year contract", "b": "An increase in accounts payable", "c": "Accelerated depreciation", "d": "A reduction in inventory", "answer": "a", "explanation": "Recognising revenue before cash is received inflates income without a matching cash inflow, a red flag for earnings quality.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Altman Z-Score is a model used to predict:", "a": "A company's stock price", "b": "The probability of corporate bankruptcy", "c": "Future dividend payments", "d": "A company's credit rating", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The Altman Z-Score uses financial ratios to estimate the probability that a firm will go bankrupt within two years.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "A company reports GHS 200m revenue, GHS 120m COGS, GHS 40m operating expenses, and GHS 10m interest expense. EBIT is:", "a": "GHS 30m", "b": "GHS 40m", "c": "GHS 80m", "d": "GHS 50m", "answer": "b", "explanation": "EBIT = Revenue - COGS - Operating expenses = 200 - 120 - 40 = GHS 40m.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Which depreciation method results in higher depreciation charges in the early years of an asset's life?", "a": "Straight-line method", "b": "Units-of-production method", "c": "Diminishing balance (declining balance) method", "d": "Sum-of-years-digits method where the asset is new", "answer": "c", "explanation": "The diminishing balance method applies a fixed rate to the reducing book value, producing higher charges in early years.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "When a company changes an accounting policy, the impact on prior periods should be:", "a": "Ignored and only reflected going forward", "b": "Restated retrospectively to ensure comparability under IFRS", "c": "Disclosed as an extraordinary item", "d": "Averaged over the next 5 years", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Under IAS 8, accounting policy changes require retrospective restatement of prior period comparatives for consistency.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "A high receivables collection period (high DSO) relative to industry peers may indicate:", "a": "The company is collecting cash faster than competitors", "b": "Lenient credit terms, collection problems, or revenue recognition issues", "c": "The company has fewer customers", "d": "Excellent customer relationships", "answer": "b", "explanation": "High DSO compared to peers may signal poor credit control, aggressive revenue recognition, or customers under financial stress.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "The operating cash flow ratio (OCF / Current liabilities) assesses:", "a": "Long-term solvency of the company", "b": "The firm's ability to cover short-term obligations from its operating cash flows", "c": "How efficiently the company uses its fixed assets", "d": "Profitability relative to asset base", "answer": "b", "explanation": "OCF ratio measures whether core operations generate sufficient cash to meet short-term liabilities, a quality liquidity metric.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Fixed asset turnover ratio measures:", "a": "The liquidity of fixed assets", "b": "Revenue generated per unit of net fixed assets", "c": "The depreciation rate of fixed assets", "d": "The proportion of fixed to current assets", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Fixed asset turnover = Revenue / Net PP&E; measures how efficiently a firm uses its fixed assets to generate sales.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "EBITDA is often used as a proxy for operating cash flow. Which of the following is a limitation of this approach?", "a": "EBITDA excludes depreciation, which is not a cash flow", "b": "EBITDA ignores changes in working capital and capital expenditure requirements", "c": "EBITDA is not reported in financial statements", "d": "EBITDA is always lower than net income", "answer": "b", "explanation": "EBITDA ignores working capital changes and CapEx needed to sustain operations, making it an imperfect cash proxy.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Which of the following signals improving operational efficiency?", "a": "Rising days payable outstanding with stable creditors", "b": "Declining inventory days and declining receivables days", "c": "Rising interest expense relative to EBIT", "d": "Falling asset turnover", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Falling DSO and inventory days mean the company is collecting cash faster and turning inventory quicker — signs of efficiency.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "The financial leverage ratio (equity multiplier) in DuPont analysis is:", "a": "Total debt / Total equity", "b": "Total assets / Shareholders' equity", "c": "Net income / Total assets", "d": "Revenue / Total assets", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Equity multiplier = Total assets / Equity; it captures the degree to which assets are financed by debt vs. equity.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Amortisation differs from depreciation in that it applies to:", "a": "Tangible fixed assets such as machinery", "b": "Intangible assets such as patents, trademarks, and goodwill (where applicable)", "c": "Current assets such as inventory", "d": "Financial investments", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Amortisation is the systematic expensing of intangible assets over their useful life, analogous to depreciation for tangibles.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "When comparing two companies, normalising earnings for non-recurring items is important because:", "a": "Non-recurring items inflate the tax charge", "b": "One-off items distort the underlying trend; removing them provides a better view of sustainable earnings", "c": "Regulators require all non-recurring items to be excluded", "d": "Non-recurring items are always fraudulent", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Normalising strips out one-off gains and losses to reveal the ongoing earnings power of the business.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "Which of the following is a sign of potentially aggressive revenue recognition?", "a": "Revenue growing broadly in line with cash collections", "b": "Rapid revenue growth alongside declining operating cash flow and rising receivables", "c": "Conservative provisioning for bad debts", "d": "Revenue declining in a recessionary environment", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Revenue growing faster than cash collections, with rising receivables and falling OCF, may indicate premature revenue recognition.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Financial Statement Analysis", "chapter_num": 2, "question": "The net debt of a company is calculated as:", "a": "Total liabilities minus shareholders' equity", "b": "Total debt (interest-bearing borrowings) minus cash and cash equivalents", "c": "Long-term debt only", "d": "Total assets minus total equity", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Net debt = Total borrowings - Cash and cash equivalents; it shows effective indebtedness after netting available cash.", "difficulty": "Easy"},

    # Chapter 3: Equity Valuation
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "The Dividend Discount Model (DDM) values a stock as:", "a": "Earnings per share divided by the required return", "b": "The present value of all expected future dividends", "c": "Book value per share plus growth rate", "d": "Revenue per share minus costs", "answer": "b", "explanation": "DDM values a stock as the PV of all future dividends discounted at the required rate of return.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "The Gordon Growth Model is applicable when:", "a": "Dividends are expected to grow at a variable rate indefinitely", "b": "Dividends are expected to grow at a constant rate indefinitely", "c": "A company pays no dividends", "d": "The company is in its early high-growth phase", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The Gordon Growth (constant growth DDM) model assumes dividends grow at a stable, constant rate in perpetuity.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "In a Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) valuation, the terminal value represents:", "a": "The book value of assets at the end of the projection period", "b": "The present value of all cash flows beyond the explicit forecast period", "c": "The total capital expenditure over the forecast period", "d": "The company's market capitalisation at year 5", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Terminal value captures the bulk of a company's value beyond the explicit forecast horizon, typically using Gordon Growth or exit multiples.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Free Cash Flow to Equity (FCFE) valuation is most appropriate for:", "a": "Valuing a firm's total enterprise value", "b": "Valuing the equity stake in a firm from the perspective of equity holders", "c": "Valuing bonds and debt instruments", "d": "Comparing firms with different capital structures", "answer": "b", "explanation": "FCFE represents cash available to equity holders after operating expenses, CapEx, working capital needs, and debt obligations.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Free Cash Flow to the Firm (FCFF) discounted at WACC gives:", "a": "The value of equity directly", "b": "The enterprise value of the firm", "c": "The value of debt", "d": "The market capitalisation", "answer": "b", "explanation": "FCFF discounted at WACC gives enterprise value (EV); to get equity value, subtract net debt from EV.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "The EV/EBITDA multiple is preferred over the P/E ratio when:", "a": "Comparing companies in the same country", "b": "Comparing companies with different capital structures, depreciation policies, or tax rates", "c": "Valuing high-growth technology firms", "d": "Valuing dividend-paying utility companies", "answer": "b", "explanation": "EV/EBITDA is capital-structure neutral and unaffected by D&A or tax differences, making it ideal for cross-company comparison.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "A company pays a current dividend of GHS 0.50, expected to grow at 8% forever. Required return is 14%. Its intrinsic value is:", "a": "GHS 7.14", "b": "GHS 9.00", "c": "GHS 8.33", "d": "GHS 6.25", "answer": "b", "explanation": "P = D1 / (r - g) = (0.50 × 1.08) / (0.14 - 0.08) = 0.54 / 0.06 = GHS 9.00.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Comparable company analysis (trading multiples) involves:", "a": "Discounting the company's cash flows at a peer-group WACC", "b": "Applying valuation multiples from comparable listed peers to the subject company's financial metrics", "c": "Using the company's own historical P/E to value current earnings", "d": "Valuing only the tangible assets of the company", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Comps valuation applies peer multiples (P/E, EV/EBITDA, P/B) to the subject firm's metrics to derive a relative value.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Precedent transaction analysis values a company based on:", "a": "The current trading multiples of listed peers", "b": "Prices paid for similar companies in past M&A transactions, typically including a control premium", "c": "The replacement cost of the firm's assets", "d": "Historical book value adjusted for inflation", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Precedent transactions use actual acquisition prices as benchmarks, incorporating control premiums paid by acquirers.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "A higher discount rate applied in a DCF valuation results in:", "a": "A higher present value of future cash flows", "b": "A lower intrinsic value estimate", "c": "No change in the intrinsic value", "d": "Higher terminal value", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A higher discount rate reduces the present value of future cash flows, lowering the intrinsic value estimate.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "The Price-to-Sales (P/S) ratio is most useful for valuing:", "a": "Mature dividend-paying companies", "b": "Companies with negative earnings or early-stage firms where profitability is not yet established", "c": "Companies with stable earnings and high dividends", "d": "Banks and financial institutions", "answer": "b", "explanation": "P/S is useful when EPS is negative or unreliable; it values the firm based on revenue generation capacity.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Residual income valuation defines residual income as:", "a": "Dividends paid above the required rate", "b": "Net income minus a charge for the equity capital employed (Net income - Cost of equity × Book equity)", "c": "Retained earnings accumulated over the years", "d": "Free cash flow minus interest payments", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Residual income = Net income - (Cost of equity × Beginning book value); it measures value added above the required equity return.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "The concept of a margin of safety in value investing means:", "a": "The portfolio is insured against losses", "b": "Buying at a significant discount to estimated intrinsic value to protect against valuation errors", "c": "Holding cash as a buffer in a portfolio", "d": "Using stop-loss orders on all equity positions", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Margin of safety is buying below intrinsic value to reduce downside risk if the valuation assumption proves incorrect.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Sensitivity of DCF value to changes in the terminal growth rate assumption is typically:", "a": "Very low because terminal value is far in the future", "b": "Very high because terminal value often represents 60-80% of total DCF value", "c": "Moderate and predictable", "d": "Zero if WACC is well-estimated", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Terminal value is the dominant component of DCF value; small changes in the terminal growth rate cause large valuation swings.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Which valuation method is most appropriate for a company that pays no dividends and has volatile cash flows?", "a": "Gordon Growth Model", "b": "Comparable company (trading multiples) analysis", "c": "Asset-based (net asset value) approach", "d": "Dividend Discount Model", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Trading multiples work regardless of dividends or cash flow volatility, making them flexible for such companies.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Control premium in M&A refers to:", "a": "A discount applied when buying a minority stake", "b": "The extra price paid above the market price to acquire a controlling stake", "c": "The management fee paid to the acquirer's advisers", "d": "The premium paid for brand intangibles", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The control premium compensates the seller for giving up decision-making control; acquirers pay more to gain strategic control.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "In a two-stage DDM, the model typically assumes:", "a": "Constant dividend growth forever", "b": "A high growth phase for several years followed by a stable perpetual growth rate", "c": "No dividends in the first stage and dividends only in the second", "d": "Declining dividends followed by growth", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Two-stage DDM models a period of above-average growth before the company matures to a stable long-run growth rate.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "The FCFF formula is:", "a": "Net income + D&A - CapEx - ΔNWC + Net borrowing", "b": "EBIT × (1 - Tax rate) + D&A - CapEx - ΔNWC", "c": "Operating income - Interest expense + Dividends", "d": "Revenue - Total costs", "answer": "b", "explanation": "FCFF = NOPAT + D&A - CapEx - ΔNWC; it is cash generated by operations available to all capital providers.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "An analyst estimates a stock's intrinsic value at GHS 8.00. The stock currently trades at GHS 10.00. The analyst should:", "a": "Issue a buy recommendation", "b": "Issue a sell/reduce recommendation as the stock appears overvalued", "c": "Issue a hold recommendation regardless", "d": "Not make a recommendation on an overvalued stock", "answer": "b", "explanation": "If the market price exceeds intrinsic value, the stock is overvalued and a sell/reduce recommendation is appropriate.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Price-to-Book (P/B) valuation is most commonly used for valuing:", "a": "Technology companies with few tangible assets", "b": "Banks and financial institutions where book value is a meaningful measure of value", "c": "Pharmaceutical companies with large intangible assets", "d": "Start-up companies", "answer": "b", "explanation": "P/B is most relevant for financial firms (banks, insurers) where assets are primarily financial and book value is meaningful.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "When using EV/EBITDA, Enterprise Value is calculated as:", "a": "Market capitalisation only", "b": "Market cap + Debt - Cash", "c": "Book value + Goodwill", "d": "EBITDA × 10", "answer": "b", "explanation": "EV = Market cap + Net debt (Total debt - Cash); it represents the total cost of acquiring the business free of cash.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "The equity risk premium for an emerging market like Ghana would typically be:", "a": "Lower than in a developed market like the US", "b": "The same as a developed market", "c": "Higher than in a developed market due to additional country risk", "d": "Negative due to high growth potential", "answer": "c", "explanation": "Emerging markets carry additional political, currency, and liquidity risk, demanding a higher equity risk premium.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Sum-of-the-parts (SOTP) valuation is most appropriate when:", "a": "A company operates in a single, homogeneous business segment", "b": "A conglomerate has multiple distinct business units that warrant separate valuation methodologies", "c": "A company has a simple capital structure", "d": "Comparable companies are widely available", "answer": "b", "explanation": "SOTP values each business segment separately and sums them to derive total enterprise value, capturing conglomerate complexity.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Equity Valuation", "chapter_num": 3, "question": "Liquidation value represents:", "a": "The present value of future earnings in a going-concern scenario", "b": "The estimated amount shareholders would receive if all assets were sold and liabilities settled", "c": "The book value of equity on the balance sheet", "d": "The price at which a controlling shareholder would sell the company", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Liquidation value is the floor value — assets sold at distressed prices minus liabilities; typically less than going-concern value.", "difficulty": "Medium"},

    # Chapter 4: Fixed Income Analysis
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Modified duration measures:", "a": "The time to maturity of a bond", "b": "The approximate percentage change in bond price for a 1% (100 bps) change in yield", "c": "The credit sensitivity of a bond", "d": "The difference between YTM and coupon rate", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Modified duration ≈ -ΔP/P ÷ Δy; it quantifies interest rate risk in terms of price sensitivity per unit yield change.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Macaulay duration is defined as:", "a": "A bond's time to maturity", "b": "The weighted average time to receive all cash flows, weighted by present value", "c": "The duration of a zero-coupon bond minus the coupon bond duration", "d": "The bond's price divided by its yield", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Macaulay duration is the PV-weighted average time to receipt of all bond cash flows; it equals maturity for zero-coupon bonds.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "A bond has a modified duration of 6 and its yield increases by 50 basis points. The approximate price change is:", "a": "+3%", "b": "-3%", "c": "+6%", "d": "-6%", "answer": "b", "explanation": "ΔP ≈ -Modified duration × Δy = -6 × 0.005 = -0.03 = -3%. Price falls by approximately 3%.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Convexity adjustment improves duration-based price estimates because:", "a": "It accounts for the linear price-yield relationship", "b": "It captures the curvature in the price-yield relationship, improving accuracy for large yield changes", "c": "It adjusts for credit risk", "d": "It accounts for accrued interest", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Duration is a linear approximation; convexity corrects for the curve, providing a more accurate estimate especially for large moves.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "The yield spread between a corporate bond and a comparable government bond widens. This most likely indicates:", "a": "Improving credit quality of the corporate issuer", "b": "Rising perceived credit risk or deteriorating market conditions for the corporate issuer", "c": "Falling market interest rates", "d": "Improving economic growth", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A widening credit spread reflects increased perceived risk of default or a broader risk-off environment for corporate bonds.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "The Z-spread on a bond is:", "a": "The spread over Treasuries at a single maturity point", "b": "A constant spread added to the entire spot rate curve to make the PV of cash flows equal the market price", "c": "The credit default swap spread", "d": "The difference between YTM and current yield", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The Z-spread is added uniformly to the zero-coupon (spot) curve; it is a more precise credit spread measure than yield spread.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Option-adjusted spread (OAS) removes the impact of embedded options from a bond's spread to give:", "a": "The credit spread for comparable option-free bonds", "b": "The total yield of the bond including options", "c": "The yield premium over the government bond", "d": "The yield of the underlying option", "answer": "a", "explanation": "OAS strips out the value of embedded options (call/put), leaving a pure credit/liquidity spread comparable to option-free bonds.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "A steepening yield curve (long rates rising faster than short rates) typically benefits:", "a": "Investors holding long-duration bonds", "b": "Banks, which can borrow short and lend long at higher spreads", "c": "Borrowers with long-term fixed rate loans", "d": "Insurance companies with long-duration liabilities", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A steeper curve widens net interest margins for banks that borrow at short-term rates and lend at longer-term rates.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Duration gap in a bank's balance sheet refers to:", "a": "The maturity mismatch between the longest and shortest assets", "b": "The difference between the duration of assets and the duration of liabilities", "c": "The time until the next interest rate reset", "d": "The gap between fixed and floating rate assets", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Duration gap measures the net interest rate risk exposure from the mismatch between asset and liability durations.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "A callable bond typically offers a higher yield than an equivalent non-callable bond because:", "a": "The issuer bears all the reinvestment risk", "b": "The investor is compensated for giving up the call option to the issuer (call risk)", "c": "Callable bonds are always investment grade", "d": "Callable bonds have shorter durations", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Investors demand a call premium (higher yield) because the issuer can refinance at lower rates, disadvantaging the bondholder.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "The bootstrapping method in yield curve construction is used to:", "a": "Estimate bond prices from historical yields", "b": "Derive zero-coupon (spot) rates from par yields of coupon-bearing bonds", "c": "Interpolate between two observable yield points", "d": "Calculate the yield spread between corporate and government bonds", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Bootstrapping sequentially derives spot rates from coupon bond prices, building the zero-coupon yield curve.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Negative convexity in a callable bond means:", "a": "The bond's price rises less than expected when yields fall due to the call option", "b": "The bond has an inverse relationship with interest rates", "c": "The bond's duration is negative", "d": "The yield curve is inverted for that maturity", "answer": "a", "explanation": "When yields fall, callable bond prices are capped because issuers will call the bond; this limits price appreciation (negative convexity).", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Credit default swaps (CDS) are used primarily to:", "a": "Lock in a fixed interest rate", "b": "Transfer credit risk from the protection buyer to the protection seller", "c": "Hedge interest rate risk on a bond portfolio", "d": "Convert floating rate bonds to fixed rate", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A CDS is a derivative where the protection seller compensates the buyer if a reference entity defaults, transferring credit risk.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Which of the following bonds has the longest duration for the same maturity?", "a": "A high-coupon bond", "b": "A low-coupon bond (closer to zero coupon)", "c": "A floating rate bond", "d": "A callable bond", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Lower coupons mean less cash is received early; more of the bond's PV is concentrated at maturity, extending duration.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "The forward rate between year 1 and year 2 implies:", "a": "The current 2-year spot rate", "b": "The expected 1-year rate starting in one year's time", "c": "The YTM of a 1-year bond", "d": "The average rate for the next 2 years", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The (1,1) forward rate is derived from spot rates and represents the market's implied 1-year rate expected to prevail in 1 year.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "A bond with a credit rating of BB is classified as:", "a": "Investment grade", "b": "Speculative grade (high yield/junk)", "c": "Risk-free", "d": "AAA equivalent", "answer": "b", "explanation": "BB is below the BBB-/Baa3 investment grade threshold; it is speculative grade, carrying higher default risk.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "The yield to worst (YTW) on a callable bond is:", "a": "The highest possible yield the investor can receive", "b": "The lowest yield the investor could receive across all possible call scenarios", "c": "The yield if the bond is held to maturity only", "d": "The yield on the worst-performing bond in a portfolio", "answer": "b", "explanation": "YTW is the minimum yield an investor would earn, calculated across all possible call dates; it represents a conservative estimate.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "In a plain vanilla interest rate swap, one party:", "a": "Exchanges one currency for another", "b": "Exchanges fixed interest payments for floating interest payments on a notional principal", "c": "Transfers credit risk to a counterparty", "d": "Receives equity dividends in exchange for bond coupons", "answer": "b", "explanation": "In a standard IRS, the fixed payer pays a fixed rate and receives a floating rate (e.g., LIBOR/SOFR) on a notional amount.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Immunisation of a bond portfolio means:", "a": "Protecting the portfolio against currency risk", "b": "Matching the duration of assets and liabilities to minimise interest rate risk", "c": "Eliminating all credit risk from the portfolio", "d": "Holding only government bonds in the portfolio", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Immunisation protects a portfolio's value from interest rate changes by matching asset and liability durations.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Ghana's domestic bond market is primarily composed of:", "a": "Corporate bonds and commercial paper", "b": "Government bonds (treasuries and notes) issued by the Ministry of Finance", "c": "Municipal bonds issued by metropolitan authorities", "d": "Eurobonds exclusively", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Ghana's domestic fixed income market is dominated by government securities issued by the Ministry of Finance through the Bank of Ghana.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "When the yield curve shifts upward in parallel by 100 basis points, a bond portfolio with duration 5 loses approximately:", "a": "1% of its value", "b": "5% of its value", "c": "10% of its value", "d": "0.5% of its value", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Price change ≈ -Duration × Δy = -5 × 0.01 = -5%. A 100 bps parallel shift causes approximately a 5% price loss.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Which of the following is a feature of a putable bond?", "a": "The issuer can repay the principal early", "b": "The investor can sell the bond back to the issuer at par before maturity", "c": "The coupon rate increases automatically if rates rise", "d": "The bond converts to equity on maturity", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A put option on a bond gives the holder the right to sell back to the issuer at a predetermined price (usually par), protecting against rising rates.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "Real yield on an inflation-linked bond is:", "a": "Nominal yield minus the current inflation rate", "b": "The yield in excess of expected inflation, representing actual purchasing power return", "c": "The yield before adjusting for taxes", "d": "The coupon divided by face value", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Real yield adjusts for inflation; inflation-linked bonds provide a return in real (purchasing power-adjusted) terms.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Fixed Income Analysis", "chapter_num": 4, "question": "The bid-offer spread in the bond market represents:", "a": "The credit spread over government bonds", "b": "The transaction cost incurred — the difference between dealer buying and selling prices", "c": "The difference between YTM and coupon rate", "d": "Annual interest payment relative to market price", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The bid-offer spread is the dealer's margin; it is a transaction cost borne by investors when buying or selling bonds.", "difficulty": "Easy"},

    # Chapter 5: Derivatives
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "A call option gives the holder the right to:", "a": "Sell an asset at the strike price before or at expiry", "b": "Buy an asset at the strike price before or at expiry", "c": "Receive a fixed payment at maturity", "d": "Exchange one currency for another", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A call option grants the holder the right (not obligation) to purchase the underlying asset at the agreed strike price.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "A put option is in-the-money when:", "a": "The underlying asset price is above the strike price", "b": "The underlying asset price is below the strike price", "c": "The underlying asset price equals the strike price", "d": "The option has no time value remaining", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A put option has intrinsic value (in-the-money) when the underlying price is below the strike, as the holder can sell above market.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "The premium of an option is composed of:", "a": "Intrinsic value only", "b": "Intrinsic value plus time value", "c": "Time value minus intrinsic value", "d": "Strike price minus market price", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Option premium = Intrinsic value (in-the-money amount) + Time value (remaining time and volatility value).", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "Delta (Δ) of an option measures:", "a": "The sensitivity of option price to changes in volatility", "b": "The rate of change of the option price relative to a one-unit change in the underlying asset price", "c": "The time decay of the option", "d": "The sensitivity of option price to changes in the risk-free rate", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Delta is the first derivative of option price with respect to the underlying price; call delta ranges 0 to 1, put delta -1 to 0.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "Theta (Θ) in options refers to:", "a": "The sensitivity to changes in the underlying price", "b": "The rate of decline in option value as time to expiry decreases (time decay)", "c": "The sensitivity to changes in volatility", "d": "The sensitivity to changes in interest rates", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Theta measures how much an option's price declines per day as expiry approaches, all else equal.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "Vega measures an option's sensitivity to:", "a": "Changes in the underlying asset price", "b": "Changes in implied volatility", "c": "Passage of time", "d": "Changes in interest rates", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Vega measures how much the option price changes per 1% change in implied volatility.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "A futures contract differs from a forward contract in that futures:", "a": "Are settled at maturity only", "b": "Are standardised, exchange-traded, and marked-to-market daily", "c": "Have no margin requirements", "d": "Can only be used for commodity hedging", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Futures are standardised exchange-traded contracts with daily mark-to-market settlement and margin requirements, unlike OTC forwards.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "A corn farmer who sells corn futures is using derivatives to:", "a": "Speculate on higher corn prices", "b": "Hedge against a fall in corn prices before harvest", "c": "Increase exposure to commodity markets", "d": "Arbitrage between spot and futures markets", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Selling futures locks in a selling price for the farmer, protecting against price falls before the harvest is sold.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "The Black-Scholes-Merton model prices European options using all of the following inputs EXCEPT:", "a": "Current underlying price", "b": "Strike price", "c": "Expected dividends over the life of the option", "d": "The option writer's credit rating", "answer": "d", "explanation": "Black-Scholes inputs are: underlying price, strike, time to expiry, risk-free rate, and volatility. Credit rating is not an input.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "Put-call parity states that:", "a": "Call premium always equals put premium for the same strike and expiry", "b": "C - P = S - PV(K), linking call, put, stock price, and present value of strike", "c": "A put and call with same maturity always have zero combined value", "d": "Call options are always more valuable than put options", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Put-call parity: C + PV(K) = P + S; a relationship that must hold to prevent arbitrage for European options.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "An investor holds a stock and buys a put option on that stock. This strategy is called:", "a": "A covered call", "b": "A protective put (portfolio insurance)", "c": "A straddle", "d": "A bull spread", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A protective put involves holding the stock and buying a put option to limit downside risk — similar to buying insurance.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "A covered call strategy involves:", "a": "Buying a call option while holding cash", "b": "Selling a call option on a stock that is already owned", "c": "Buying both a call and put at the same strike", "d": "Shorting a stock and buying a put", "answer": "b", "explanation": "In a covered call, an investor holding a stock sells a call option, earning premium income but capping upside potential.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "An interest rate cap is a derivatives contract that:", "a": "Sets a minimum interest rate for a borrower", "b": "Protects a borrower against interest rates rising above a specified cap rate", "c": "Fixes an exchange rate for a future transaction", "d": "Exchanges fixed for floating interest payments", "answer": "b", "explanation": "An interest rate cap protects floating rate borrowers; the seller pays the buyer when rates exceed the cap level.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "The initial margin in a futures contract is:", "a": "The total value of the futures contract", "b": "A good-faith deposit required to open a futures position", "c": "The daily settlement payment", "d": "The transaction fee charged by the exchange", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Initial margin is the performance bond deposited when entering a futures contract; it is a fraction of the contract's total value.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "A currency swap involves:", "a": "Exchanging interest payments in the same currency at different rates", "b": "Exchanging principal and interest payments in one currency for principal and interest in another", "c": "Buying currency options to hedge exchange rate risk", "d": "Speculating on short-term currency movements", "answer": "b", "explanation": "In a currency swap, two parties exchange cash flows (including principal) in different currencies over the term of the agreement.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "Gamma (Γ) in options trading measures:", "a": "Time decay of the option", "b": "The rate of change of delta relative to changes in the underlying price", "c": "Sensitivity to volatility changes", "d": "Sensitivity to interest rate changes", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Gamma is the second derivative of option price with respect to the underlying; high gamma means delta changes rapidly.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "A straddle options strategy profits when:", "a": "The underlying price remains unchanged", "b": "The underlying price moves significantly in either direction", "c": "Implied volatility decreases", "d": "The market is bearish only", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A long straddle (buy call + buy put at same strike) profits when the underlying moves far enough to recover premium costs.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "The main risk of selling (writing) a naked call option is:", "a": "Limited to the premium received", "b": "Potentially unlimited, as the underlying can rise indefinitely", "c": "Loss of the initial margin only", "d": "Loss limited to the strike price minus the premium", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A naked call writer has no underlying position to offset; if the price rises sharply, losses are theoretically unlimited.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "Basis risk in hedging with futures refers to:", "a": "The risk that the futures price diverges from the expected spot price", "b": "The risk that the credit quality of the futures counterparty changes", "c": "The risk of margin calls disrupting the hedge", "d": "The risk that futures contracts expire before the hedging period ends", "answer": "a", "explanation": "Basis risk is the residual risk that remains because the futures price and spot price do not move in perfect correlation.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "An equity index futures contract can be used by a portfolio manager to:", "a": "Eliminate all portfolio risk", "b": "Gain or reduce market exposure without trading the underlying stocks directly", "c": "Earn dividends on the index stocks", "d": "Guarantee a minimum portfolio return", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Equity index futures allow efficient adjustment of market exposure (beta hedging) without transacting in individual stocks.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "The fair value (theoretical price) of a forward contract on a non-dividend-paying stock is:", "a": "S × e^(-rT)", "b": "S × e^(rT)", "c": "K × e^(-rT)", "d": "K / S × e^(rT)", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Forward price F = S × e^(rT) (continuous compounding) or F = S × (1+r)^T; it reflects the spot price carried forward at the risk-free rate.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "Which of the following is NOT a use of derivatives?", "a": "Hedging an existing risk exposure", "b": "Speculating on asset price movements", "c": "Arbitraging price differences across markets", "d": "Eliminating all forms of investment risk permanently", "answer": "d", "explanation": "Derivatives hedge, speculate, or arbitrage but cannot permanently eliminate all forms of risk — they transfer risk between parties.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Derivatives", "chapter_num": 5, "question": "Counterparty risk is higher in which of the following?", "a": "Exchange-traded futures", "b": "OTC (over-the-counter) derivatives", "c": "Listed options", "d": "Standardised futures with central clearing", "answer": "b", "explanation": "OTC derivatives involve direct bilateral agreements without central clearing, creating counterparty default risk absent in exchange-traded instruments.", "difficulty": "Medium"},

    # Chapter 6: Portfolio Management
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT) was developed by:", "a": "William Sharpe", "b": "Harry Markowitz", "c": "Eugene Fama", "d": "Fischer Black", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Harry Markowitz introduced MPT in 1952, showing how diversification can optimise portfolio risk and return.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The efficient frontier represents portfolios that:", "a": "Have the highest risk for a given level of return", "b": "Offer the maximum expected return for each level of risk", "c": "Include only risk-free assets", "d": "Minimise transaction costs", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The efficient frontier is the set of mean-variance optimal portfolios — maximum return for each risk level.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The Capital Market Line (CML) represents:", "a": "The set of all possible portfolios of risky assets", "b": "The risk-return combinations achievable by combining the market portfolio with the risk-free asset", "c": "The relationship between a single asset's beta and its expected return", "d": "The efficient frontier without the risk-free asset", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The CML shows portfolios formed by combining the tangency (market) portfolio with risk-free borrowing/lending.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The Security Market Line (SML) in CAPM represents:", "a": "The relationship between standard deviation and expected return", "b": "The relationship between systematic risk (beta) and expected return for all assets", "c": "The efficient frontier for risky assets only", "d": "The combination of risk-free and risky assets", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The SML plots expected return against beta; securities plot on the SML when fairly priced according to CAPM.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "A portfolio's alpha in a CAPM framework is:", "a": "Its total return", "b": "Its excess return above the CAPM-predicted return for its level of systematic risk", "c": "Its standard deviation", "d": "Its Sharpe ratio", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Alpha measures manager skill — returns above what CAPM would predict given the portfolio's beta exposure.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Strategic asset allocation (SAA) refers to:", "a": "Making tactical short-term bets on market movements", "b": "Setting long-term target allocations to asset classes based on investor objectives and risk tolerance", "c": "Rebalancing a portfolio daily to maintain target weights", "d": "Selecting individual securities within each asset class", "answer": "b", "explanation": "SAA establishes the long-run benchmark allocation that reflects the investor's objectives, time horizon, and risk tolerance.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Tactical asset allocation (TAA) involves:", "a": "Setting the long-term strategic benchmark weights", "b": "Making short-term deviations from SAA weights to exploit market opportunities", "c": "Rebalancing the portfolio when allocations drift beyond tolerance bands", "d": "Selecting the investment policy statement parameters", "answer": "b", "explanation": "TAA involves active, short-term tilts away from the SAA in response to market views or valuation opportunities.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The Sharpe ratio of a portfolio is 0.8 when the risk-free rate is 5%, portfolio return is 13%, and standard deviation is 10%. This is calculated as:", "a": "(13% - 5%) / 10% = 0.8", "b": "13% / 10% = 1.3", "c": "(13% + 5%) / 10% = 1.8", "d": "5% / 10% = 0.5", "answer": "a", "explanation": "Sharpe ratio = (Portfolio return - Rf) / Std dev = (13% - 5%) / 10% = 8% / 10% = 0.8.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Information ratio measures:", "a": "Portfolio return relative to benchmark return", "b": "Active return (portfolio return minus benchmark return) per unit of tracking error", "c": "The ratio of information available to the portfolio manager", "d": "Alpha divided by the market return", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Information ratio = Active return / Tracking error; it measures consistency of active performance relative to risk taken.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Rebalancing a portfolio back to target weights is necessary because:", "a": "Asset prices change, causing actual weights to drift from strategic targets", "b": "Tax regulations require annual portfolio changes", "c": "New regulations mandate portfolio adjustments", "d": "Fees compound over time and erode allocations", "answer": "a", "explanation": "As asset prices change, portfolio weights drift from targets; rebalancing restores the intended risk profile.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The minimum variance portfolio on the efficient frontier is:", "a": "The portfolio with the highest Sharpe ratio", "b": "The portfolio with the lowest possible standard deviation among all efficient portfolios", "c": "The risk-free asset", "d": "The market portfolio", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The minimum variance portfolio is the leftmost point on the efficient frontier, having the lowest achievable standard deviation.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Benchmark-relative investing means a portfolio manager aims to:", "a": "Achieve absolute positive returns at all times", "b": "Outperform a designated market benchmark index", "c": "Match the risk-free rate of return", "d": "Minimise transaction costs regardless of returns", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Benchmark-relative managers measure success against an index; alpha is the value added above the benchmark.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Tracking error in portfolio management is:", "a": "The error made in pricing securities", "b": "The standard deviation of the difference between portfolio returns and benchmark returns", "c": "The commission charged by the broker", "d": "The gap between NAV and market price", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Tracking error measures how closely a portfolio follows its benchmark; low tracking error is characteristic of index funds.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The Treynor ratio is most appropriate for comparing:", "a": "Standalone portfolio performance ignoring diversification", "b": "Well-diversified portfolios where unsystematic risk is negligible", "c": "Portfolios of only government bonds", "d": "Short-term trading strategies", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Treynor uses beta (systematic risk), appropriate for well-diversified portfolios where unsystematic risk has been eliminated.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Factor investing (smart beta) exploits:", "a": "Random price movements in the stock market", "b": "Systematic return premia associated with factors like value, momentum, size, and quality", "c": "Inside information about company earnings", "d": "Arbitrage between different exchanges", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Factor investing targets documented risk premia (value, momentum, low volatility, etc.) that have historically delivered excess returns.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Core-satellite portfolio construction combines:", "a": "Equity and bond allocations only", "b": "A passive index core with active satellite positions targeting alpha", "c": "Domestic and international allocations", "d": "Short-term and long-term bonds", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Core-satellite uses a low-cost passive core for market exposure while active satellite allocations seek to add alpha.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The investment policy statement (IPS) should be reviewed when:", "a": "The market falls by more than 5%", "b": "There are significant changes in the investor's circumstances, objectives, or constraints", "c": "The portfolio manager changes", "d": "Quarterly, as a regulatory requirement", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The IPS should be updated when the client's situation changes significantly (e.g., life events, risk tolerance changes).", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Dollar-cost averaging reduces the average cost of investment because:", "a": "It always buys at the lowest price", "b": "It buys more units when prices are low and fewer when high, reducing the average purchase price", "c": "It eliminates market timing entirely", "d": "It invests only in cheap securities", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Investing fixed amounts regularly buys more shares when prices fall and fewer when prices rise, lowering the average cost.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The coefficient of variation (CV) is used in portfolio analysis to:", "a": "Measure absolute risk", "b": "Compare risk per unit of expected return across assets with different return levels", "c": "Calculate the correlation between two assets", "d": "Determine the optimal portfolio weights", "answer": "b", "explanation": "CV = Standard deviation / Mean return; it standardises risk by expected return, enabling comparison across different-return assets.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Liability-driven investment (LDI) strategy is commonly used by:", "a": "Individual retail investors seeking growth", "b": "Pension funds and insurers matching assets to fund specific future liabilities", "c": "Hedge funds seeking absolute returns", "d": "Banks managing their loan portfolios", "answer": "b", "explanation": "LDI matches asset cash flows and duration to specific future liabilities (e.g., pension payouts), minimising surplus risk.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Mean-variance optimisation requires estimates of:", "a": "Asset prices and dividend yields only", "b": "Expected returns, variances, and covariances for all assets considered", "c": "Historical prices going back at least 20 years", "d": "Credit ratings and liquidity scores", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Markowitz optimisation needs expected returns, standard deviations (or variances), and correlations (covariances) as inputs.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "The tangency portfolio on the efficient frontier is special because:", "a": "It has zero variance", "b": "It is the portfolio that maximises the Sharpe ratio", "c": "It contains only risk-free assets", "d": "It has the highest expected return", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The tangency portfolio is where the CML touches the efficient frontier; it is the market portfolio and has the highest Sharpe ratio.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Which risk measure captures only the downside risk of a portfolio?", "a": "Standard deviation", "b": "Beta", "c": "Semi-variance or downside deviation", "d": "Coefficient of variation", "answer": "c", "explanation": "Semi-variance/downside deviation measures only negative deviations from the mean or target, capturing asymmetric (downside) risk.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "An endowment fund's primary investment objective is typically:", "a": "Capital preservation with no growth", "b": "Generating sufficient returns to support the institution's spending needs while preserving real capital", "c": "Maximising short-term income", "d": "Matching the return of a specific benchmark exactly", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Endowments must earn enough to fund annual spending (often 4-5% of assets) while preserving capital in real terms for perpetuity.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Portfolio Management", "chapter_num": 6, "question": "Which of the following best describes a passive investment strategy?", "a": "Selecting stocks based on deep fundamental analysis to beat the market", "b": "Replicating the composition and returns of a market index at minimal cost", "c": "Using leverage to amplify market returns", "d": "Rotating between sectors based on macroeconomic analysis", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Passive investing tracks a market index, accepting market returns while minimising costs and turnover.", "difficulty": "Easy"},

    # Chapter 7: Risk Management
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Value at Risk (VaR) at a 95% confidence level over 1 day of GHS 1 million means:", "a": "The portfolio will never lose more than GHS 1 million", "b": "There is a 5% probability of losing more than GHS 1 million in a single day", "c": "The portfolio is expected to lose GHS 1 million per year", "d": "The maximum daily loss is exactly GHS 1 million", "answer": "b", "explanation": "95% VaR means in 5% of days (1 in 20), losses are expected to exceed GHS 1 million — it is a threshold, not a maximum.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Expected Shortfall (ES), also known as Conditional VaR (CVaR), measures:", "a": "The maximum possible loss", "b": "The average loss in scenarios that exceed the VaR threshold", "c": "The probability of default", "d": "The volatility of the portfolio", "answer": "b", "explanation": "CVaR/ES is the expected loss given that losses exceed the VaR level; it captures tail risk better than VaR alone.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Stress testing in risk management involves:", "a": "Testing the physical security of bank infrastructure", "b": "Evaluating portfolio performance under extreme but plausible adverse scenarios", "c": "Calculating daily VaR under normal market conditions", "d": "Measuring credit exposure to individual counterparties", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Stress tests assess resilience under severe scenarios (market crashes, rate shocks) that may not be captured by VaR models.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Credit risk management focuses on:", "a": "Managing the risk of share price declines", "b": "Assessing and managing the risk of counterparty default or credit quality deterioration", "c": "Controlling the interest rate sensitivity of a bond portfolio", "d": "Reducing operational errors in transaction processing", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Credit risk encompasses default risk, downgrade risk, and credit spread risk arising from counterparty or issuer obligations.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Market risk refers to:", "a": "The risk of counterparty default", "b": "The risk of losses from adverse movements in market prices (equities, rates, FX, commodities)", "c": "The risk of operational failures", "d": "The risk of regulatory non-compliance", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Market risk arises from movements in equity prices, interest rates, foreign exchange rates, and commodity prices.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Operational risk is defined by Basel as:", "a": "The risk of loss from market movements", "b": "The risk of loss from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, systems, or external events", "c": "The risk that a bank's credit rating is downgraded", "d": "The risk of liquidity shortfalls", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Basel II/III defines operational risk as the risk of loss from internal failures (processes, systems, people) or external events.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Which of the following is a limitation of the historical simulation approach to VaR?", "a": "It does not use actual market data", "b": "It assumes the past distribution of returns will repeat in the future", "c": "It cannot accommodate fat-tailed distributions", "d": "It requires complex Monte Carlo simulations", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Historical simulation assumes past return distributions represent the future, missing risks in regimes not seen in the historical window.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "The Basel III regulatory framework requires banks to maintain:", "a": "A minimum leverage ratio and liquidity coverage ratio (LCR)", "b": "Only a minimum equity capital ratio", "c": "A fixed interest rate on all loans", "d": "A specific credit rating from a recognised agency", "answer": "a", "explanation": "Basel III introduced the leverage ratio (non-risk-based), LCR (short-term liquidity), and NSFR (stable funding) on top of capital ratios.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) requires banks to hold:", "a": "Sufficient loan loss provisions for expected credit losses", "b": "High-quality liquid assets to cover 30 days of net cash outflows in a stress scenario", "c": "Minimum equity capital equal to 8% of risk-weighted assets", "d": "A maximum loan-to-deposit ratio of 80%", "answer": "b", "explanation": "LCR = HQLA / Net Cash Outflows over 30 days ≥ 100%; ensures short-term liquidity resilience under a stress scenario.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Delta-normal VaR is calculated using:", "a": "Actual historical returns from the past 250 days", "b": "The assumption that portfolio returns are normally distributed, using portfolio delta and volatility", "c": "Monte Carlo simulation of 10,000 scenarios", "d": "The worst historical loss observed in the dataset", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Delta-normal (parametric) VaR assumes normality and uses portfolio sensitivity (delta) and volatility to estimate potential loss.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Concentration risk in a credit portfolio arises from:", "a": "Holding too many small loans", "b": "Excessive exposure to a single borrower, sector, or geography", "c": "Underestimating market volatility", "d": "Too much diversification reducing returns", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Concentration risk is the exposure to credit losses if a large single borrower or related group defaults.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "A risk management framework typically includes all of the following EXCEPT:", "a": "Risk identification and measurement", "b": "Risk monitoring and reporting", "c": "Risk mitigation and control", "d": "Guaranteeing zero losses in all circumstances", "answer": "d", "explanation": "Risk management aims to identify, measure, monitor, and mitigate risks; it cannot guarantee zero losses.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Systemic risk in the financial system refers to:", "a": "The risk faced by a single firm", "b": "The risk of a breakdown in the entire financial system due to the failure of interconnected institutions", "c": "The aggregate of all individual credit risks", "d": "The risk arising from natural disasters", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Systemic risk is the risk of contagion — one failure triggering widespread financial instability across the interconnected system.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Wrong-way risk in counterparty credit risk occurs when:", "a": "A firm's credit exposure and counterparty credit quality deteriorate simultaneously", "b": "Market risk increases unexpectedly", "c": "The collateral posted by the counterparty declines in value", "d": "A hedging position moves against the hedger", "answer": "a", "explanation": "Wrong-way risk is when exposure to a counterparty increases at the same time as their credit quality deteriorates.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Model risk arises from:", "a": "Using models that correctly price all financial instruments", "b": "Errors or limitations in financial models used for pricing, hedging, or risk measurement", "c": "Over-reliance on qualitative judgment without models", "d": "Using too many models simultaneously", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Model risk is the risk of loss from using a flawed or misspecified model; all models have simplifying assumptions.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Duration management in a bond portfolio is a form of:", "a": "Credit risk management", "b": "Interest rate risk management", "c": "Operational risk management", "d": "Liquidity risk management", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Managing portfolio duration controls the sensitivity of the bond portfolio's value to interest rate changes.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "The probability of default (PD) in credit risk models represents:", "a": "The loss if the borrower defaults", "b": "The likelihood that a borrower will fail to meet its debt obligations", "c": "The percentage of the exposure recovered after default", "d": "The number of days past due on a loan", "answer": "b", "explanation": "PD is the statistical probability that a counterparty will default over a given time horizon.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Loss Given Default (LGD) measures:", "a": "The total amount owed at the time of default", "b": "The proportion of the exposure that is lost when a borrower defaults (1 - Recovery rate)", "c": "The probability that the borrower will default", "d": "The time it takes to recover assets after default", "answer": "b", "explanation": "LGD = 1 - Recovery rate; it measures the percentage of exposure lost assuming default has occurred.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Exposure at Default (EAD) in credit risk represents:", "a": "The probability of default multiplied by LGD", "b": "The total exposure the lender faces if the borrower defaults at that point in time", "c": "The market value of collateral pledged by the borrower", "d": "The loan-to-value ratio of a secured loan", "answer": "b", "explanation": "EAD is the outstanding amount owed at the time of default, including drawn amounts and estimated undrawn exposures.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) differs from traditional siloed risk management by:", "a": "Focusing only on financial risks", "b": "Taking a holistic, integrated view of all risks across the entire organisation", "c": "Delegating risk management exclusively to the board", "d": "Focusing on operational risk only", "answer": "b", "explanation": "ERM integrates all risk categories (financial, operational, strategic, reputational) into a unified firm-wide framework.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "A risk heat map is used to:", "a": "Model interest rate risk across the yield curve", "b": "Visualise and prioritise risks based on their likelihood and potential impact", "c": "Map counterparty credit exposures in a network", "d": "Display portfolio alpha and beta over time", "answer": "b", "explanation": "A risk heat map plots risks on a probability-vs-impact matrix, helping organisations prioritise risk mitigation efforts.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Scenario analysis in risk management differs from sensitivity analysis in that:", "a": "Scenario analysis changes one variable at a time", "b": "Scenario analysis examines the combined impact of multiple risk factors changing simultaneously", "c": "Sensitivity analysis uses historical simulation only", "d": "Scenario analysis is only used for equity portfolios", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Scenario analysis models realistic combinations of simultaneous adverse changes across multiple risk drivers.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "Which of the following best mitigates counterparty credit risk in OTC derivatives?", "a": "Buying more options", "b": "Using standardised contracts and central clearing through a central counterparty (CCP)", "c": "Increasing the notional size of the position", "d": "Shortening the maturity of the contract", "answer": "b", "explanation": "CCP clearing interposes a central counterparty between bilateral OTC parties, reducing counterparty default risk through netting and margin.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Risk Management", "chapter_num": 7, "question": "The risk appetite statement of an organisation:", "a": "Sets daily trading limits for individual traders", "b": "Articulates the type and amount of risk the organisation is willing to accept in pursuit of its objectives", "c": "Determines the required regulatory capital", "d": "Lists all risks the organisation faces", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The risk appetite statement guides decision-making by defining how much risk the board and management are prepared to take.", "difficulty": "Medium"},

    # Chapter 8: Alternative Investments
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Alternative investments are generally characterised by:", "a": "High liquidity and low transaction costs", "b": "Low correlation with traditional asset classes, limited liquidity, and less regulation", "c": "Guaranteed returns and capital protection", "d": "Being exclusively available to retail investors", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Alternatives (private equity, hedge funds, real estate, commodities) tend to have lower correlation, less liquidity, and less transparency than traditional assets.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Private equity involves investing in:", "a": "Shares of publicly listed companies on a stock exchange", "b": "Equity of companies that are not publicly listed", "c": "Government bonds and treasury bills", "d": "Public mutual funds", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Private equity invests in the equity of private (unlisted) companies, often through buyouts, venture capital, or growth equity.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "A leveraged buyout (LBO) involves:", "a": "Acquiring a company using primarily equity financing", "b": "Acquiring a company using a significant proportion of debt financing, with the acquired company's assets as collateral", "c": "Listing a private company through an IPO", "d": "Merging two companies of equal size", "answer": "b", "explanation": "LBOs use large amounts of debt to finance the acquisition, with the target's cash flows and assets servicing the debt.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Venture capital (VC) investments typically target:", "a": "Mature, dividend-paying companies", "b": "Early-stage or growth-stage companies with high growth potential but high risk", "c": "Distressed companies undergoing restructuring", "d": "Large-cap companies seeking additional capital", "answer": "b", "explanation": "VC provides capital to early-stage companies with high growth potential in exchange for equity, accepting high failure rates.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "A hedge fund's '2 and 20' fee structure means:", "a": "2% performance fee and 20% management fee", "b": "2% annual management fee and 20% performance fee on profits", "c": "2% upfront load and 20% redemption fee", "d": "2% fixed return and 20% equity participation", "answer": "b", "explanation": "'2 and 20' is the traditional hedge fund fee model: 2% of AUM as management fee + 20% of any investment profits as performance fee.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Real estate investment can provide which of the following benefits?", "a": "High liquidity and low management costs", "b": "Inflation hedge, income through rents, and portfolio diversification", "c": "Zero correlation with all other asset classes", "d": "Guaranteed appreciation in value", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Real estate provides rental income, potential capital appreciation, inflation protection, and diversification benefits.", "difficulty": "Easy"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Cap rate (capitalisation rate) in real estate is:", "a": "The mortgage interest rate", "b": "Net operating income divided by property value — a measure of property yield", "c": "The maximum rent that can be charged", "d": "The rate of property price appreciation", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Cap rate = NOI / Property value; it measures the unlevered yield on a property investment.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Commodity investments can provide which of the following benefits in a portfolio?", "a": "Regular dividend income", "b": "Inflation protection and diversification from financial assets", "c": "Capital guarantee", "d": "Tax-free returns", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Commodities tend to rise with inflation and are less correlated with stocks and bonds, providing diversification benefits.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "A high-water mark in hedge fund performance fees means:", "a": "The hedge fund's maximum historic return", "b": "The fund must recover previous losses before charging a performance fee on new gains", "c": "The minimum return required before any fee is charged", "d": "The fund's maximum allowable leverage level", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The high-water mark ensures managers only earn performance fees on net new profits above the previous peak NAV.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Infrastructure investments (e.g., toll roads, ports, power plants) are attractive to institutional investors because:", "a": "They offer high short-term trading returns", "b": "They provide long-term, inflation-linked cash flows with monopolistic characteristics", "c": "They have high liquidity and low correlation with interest rates", "d": "They are exempt from all forms of regulation", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Infrastructure assets provide stable, long-duration, inflation-linked cash flows that match institutional investor liabilities.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "The J-curve effect in private equity means:", "a": "Returns are highest in the early years of a fund", "b": "Funds typically show negative returns in early years due to fees and invested capital, then positive returns as investments mature", "c": "Investment performance follows a normal distribution", "d": "Leverage creates exponentially increasing returns", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The J-curve describes how PE funds show initial negative returns (fees, early-stage losses) before generating positive returns as portfolio companies grow.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Fund of hedge funds (FoHFs) provide investors with:", "a": "Direct investment in a single hedge fund strategy", "b": "Diversification across multiple hedge fund strategies with professional manager selection", "c": "Guaranteed returns above money market rates", "d": "Daily liquidity equivalent to money market funds", "answer": "b", "explanation": "FoHFs invest in a portfolio of hedge funds, providing diversification across strategies and professional due diligence.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "The internal rate of return (IRR) is the primary return metric used in:", "a": "Mutual fund performance reporting", "b": "Private equity fund performance reporting", "c": "Bond yield calculations", "d": "Real estate rental yield calculations", "answer": "b", "explanation": "IRR is the standard PE performance metric, capturing the timing of capital calls and distributions over the fund's life.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Illiquidity premium in alternative investments refers to:", "a": "Additional fees charged for illiquid investments", "b": "The extra return investors demand to compensate for the inability to exit the investment quickly", "c": "A penalty for early redemption from a hedge fund", "d": "The discount applied to the market value of illiquid assets", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Illiquidity premium compensates investors for the risk of not being able to sell an investment quickly at fair value.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Which of the following hedge fund strategies seeks to profit from price discrepancies between related securities?", "a": "Global macro", "b": "Long/short equity", "c": "Relative value arbitrage", "d": "Managed futures", "answer": "c", "explanation": "Relative value strategies exploit pricing inefficiencies between related instruments (e.g., convertible bonds, paired equities).", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Ghana's pension funds are permitted to invest in alternative assets under:", "a": "No restriction — they can invest in any asset", "b": "NPRA guidelines that cap allocations to alternative assets", "c": "Bank of Ghana directives only", "d": "SEC Ghana rules for CIS only", "answer": "b", "explanation": "The National Pensions Regulatory Authority (NPRA) sets investment guidelines including limits on alternative assets for pension funds.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Net Asset Value (NAV) per share in private equity is typically:", "a": "Quoted daily on a public exchange", "b": "Based on unrealised (estimated) valuations of portfolio companies until exit", "c": "Calculated using the market prices of all holdings", "d": "Fixed at the initial subscription price", "answer": "b", "explanation": "PE fund NAV is based on estimated fair values of portfolio companies; true returns are only known at exit (realisation).", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "A total return swap on a real estate index allows an investor to:", "a": "Buy properties directly", "b": "Gain synthetic exposure to real estate returns without owning physical property", "c": "Hedge rental income from existing properties", "d": "Convert property capital gains to income returns", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Total return swaps on real estate indices provide synthetic exposure, allowing investors to gain/reduce real estate allocation without physical transactions.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Alternative Investments", "chapter_num": 8, "question": "Which of the following is a key risk specific to real estate investment?", "a": "Counterparty risk from derivatives exposure", "b": "Illiquidity, physical management requirements, and concentration in specific locations", "c": "Daily mark-to-market losses from exchange-traded prices", "d": "Regulatory restrictions on dividends", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Real estate risks include illiquidity, location concentration, physical maintenance, and dependence on local economic conditions.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
