    {"chapter": "Regulatory Framework", "chapter_num": 10, "question": "The concept of regulatory arbitrage refers to:", "a": "Using regulatory differences to earn risk-free profits in securities markets", "b": "Exploiting differences in regulations across jurisdictions to reduce regulatory burden or costs", "c": "A legal practice endorsed by all regulators", "d": "The process of appealing a regulatory decision", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Regulatory arbitrage occurs when firms exploit differences in regulations between jurisdictions or sectors to reduce compliance costs or obligations.", "difficulty": "Hard"},
    {"chapter": "Regulatory Framework", "chapter_num": 10, "question": "Under the SEC Ghana's CIS Regulations, a fund manager must maintain:", "a": "Minimum assets under management of GHS 10 million", "b": "A minimum stated capital as specified by SEC Ghana and operational separation between fund assets and firm assets", "c": "An office in at least three regions of Ghana", "d": "A fixed dividend policy approved by the SEC", "answer": "b", "explanation": "SEC Ghana sets minimum capital and operational requirements for fund managers, including segregation of client assets.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
    {"chapter": "Regulatory Framework", "chapter_num": 10, "question": "The principle of regulatory proportionality means:", "a": "All firms face identical regulatory requirements regardless of size", "b": "Regulatory requirements should be proportionate to the size, complexity, and risk profile of the regulated entity", "c": "Small firms pay lower taxes", "d": "Regulation increases proportionally with company profits", "answer": "b", "explanation": "Proportionality tailors regulatory intensity to the nature and scale of an institution's activities and associated risks.", "difficulty": "Medium"},
